Hello, and welcome to TechScape. I=E2=80=99m Blake Montgomery, US tech edit=
or at the Guardian, writing to you while moving laundry from the washer to =
the dryer, wishing a robot could do the task for me. Today in tech, we=E2=
=80=99re talking about workers and AI.

Workers under surveillance

Employees across the globe are increasingly finding themselves minutely mon=
itored in their workplaces, with tiny changes in their behaviors resulting =
in negative performance evaluations.

The latest example of workers under surveillance and bristling at it comes =
from Co-op, which operates supermarkets, undertakers, insurance and legal s=
ervices companies in the UK. The company implemented an automated listening=
 technology that rates every phone call some of them make to customers.

In a system described as =E2=80=9Coppressive and dystopian=E2=80=9D by a wh=
istleblower, Co-op Legal Services is using AI models to record, analyze and=
 award a percentage score for interactions between agents and customers see=
king advice about probate, wills and estates.

=E2=80=9CYou are being watched,=E2=80=9D said a Co-op worker who spoke to t=
he Guardian on condition of anonymity.

