The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind

Across AI-related occupations, the median pay of men is $45,000 higher than=
 that of women, partly due to the types of jobs men and women are more like=
ly to have, according to research by LinkedIn: men are still typically hire=
d for higher-paying roles in the AI industry.

When pitching investors, one female co-founder of an AI startup said she ha=
d found through trial and error that she and her male co-founder are more l=
ikely to get an investment if he does the pitching.

Another story in the series examines how dire working conditions can become=
 under the pressures of AI and automation. Refugees in Kenya are completing=
 tasks for foreign technology companies under bizarre and opaque conditions=
.

The stories of desperate remote data workers used to highlight the low pay =
and horrid content they were exposed to. Today, workers who spoke to the Gu=
ardian say they aren=E2=80=99t even sure they will be paid, as their compen=
sation is tied to a so-called =E2=80=9Creward=E2=80=9D system that decides =
whether they receive payment based on the quality of their work. Many of th=
e workers called the system unfair but also said with dismay that they had =
few, if any, other options.

